9. The Tories Betrayed Britain

Now we reach the genuinely beautiful bit.

On 1 September 2026, Goodwin shared an article saying:

“If you want to know just how badly the Tories betrayed Britain…”

The proposition is familiar throughout Reform politics.

The Conservatives were not merely unsuccessful.

They betrayed the country.

The language matters.

Because “failure” allows examination of the ideas.

“Betrayal” protects them.

Consider two competing explanations.

Hypothesis A

The Conservative Party implemented or tolerated policies that produced outcomes its voters disliked, and therefore some of those political assumptions may require reconsideration.

Hypothesis B

The ideas were basically right.

The personnel betrayed them.

Hypothesis B has an enormous political advantage.

Nothing fundamental ever needs changing.

The ideology remains pristine.

Only the staff were contaminated.

Thus:

[
\text{Conservative government}
+
\text{undesirable outcome}
]

does not yield:

[
\text{reconsider political programme}.
]

Instead:

[
\text{Conservative government}
+
\text{undesirable outcome}

\text{not enough proper conservatism}.
]

The cure is therefore obvious.

More.

