10. Reform UK’s Radical Solution to the Tory Problem: Acquire the Tories

This would already be funny.

Then we inspect Reform’s personnel.

As of 1 September 2026, UK Parliament lists eight Reform UK MPs:

* Lee Anderson
* Suella Braverman
* Nigel Farage
* Robert Jenrick
* Danny Kruger
* Sarah Pochin
* Andrew Rosindell
* Richard Tice. (UK Parliament)

The Institute for Government notes that five of those eight sitting Reform MPs were previously Conservative MPs. (Institute for Government)

Those five include people such as former Home Secretary Suella Braverman, former immigration minister and housing secretary Robert Jenrick, Danny Kruger, Lee Anderson and Andrew Rosindell. Parliament’s own records show, for example, Jenrick moving from Conservative affiliation to Reform in January 2026, Kruger doing so in September 2025, Rosindell in January 2026 and Braverman later that same month. (UK Parliament)

Fine.

Perhaps the remaining three represent the great revolutionary break with Toryism.

Unfortunately:

Nigel Farage was previously a Conservative Party member, leaving in 1992 over Maastricht before helping establish UKIP. (The Guardian)

Richard Tice states on his own website that he was a Conservative member for “much of my adult life” until April 2019. (Richard Tice)

And Sarah Pochin previously served as a Conservative councillor between 2015 and 2020. (Politics Home)

So the current Reform Commons delegation produces this magnificent result:

[
\boxed{
8/8
}
]

have some previous Conservative Party membership or representation history.

Not eight former Tory MPs.

That would be inaccurate.

Five are former Conservative MPs.

The other three nevertheless come with their own previous Conservative membership or elected-service histories.

It is less a clean political rupture than a house move.

