13. The Ship of Theseus, Except Everyone Used to Be Tory

Reform describes itself as an insurgency against the political establishment.

Yet significant portions of its senior parliamentary personnel were themselves participants in that establishment.

This raises a version of the ancient Ship of Theseus problem.

Suppose you take a political party.

You replace the logo.

You replace the name.

You add Nigel Farage.

Then, one by one, you begin installing people who served as Conservative MPs, Conservative ministers, a Conservative Home Secretary, Conservative councillors and lifelong Conservative members.

At what precise percentage of former Tories does the vessel become Tory again?

Political philosophy has unfortunately neglected this question.

Reform maintains that it is not simply Conservative Party 2.0. Farage explicitly rejected that description while the defections were occurring. (ITVX)

This makes the empirical question even funnier.

Because if Reform is not Tory Party 2.0, it has nevertheless imported an impressive quantity of Tory Party 1.0 source code.

