14. Betrayal as Ideological Antivirus Software

This explains why the word betrayal is so useful.

If Conservative government produced disappointing outcomes because conservative ideas were wrong, Reform inherits a problem.

Many of Reform’s personnel and much of its voter coalition emerged from the same political family.

But if Conservative government failed because the Conservatives betrayed conservatism, Reform inherits no problem whatsoever.

It inherits the voters.

The argument becomes:

You were right.

Your beliefs were right.

Your instincts were right.

You didn’t make a bad political choice.

They betrayed you.

This is psychologically magnificent.

Nobody buying the product has to reconsider anything.

There was no failed experiment.

There were only treacherous laboratory technicians.

And, conveniently, several of those technicians have now crossed the floor and joined Reform.

Apparently betrayal has a surprisingly short half-life.

