18. Is Matt Goodwin Stupid?

This is actually the least interesting question.

Goodwin has a doctorate, held an academic post and has written serious scholarly work.

Calling him simply unintelligent lets the arguments escape examination.

The more damaging question is:

Are the arguments intelligent?

A clever person is perfectly capable of producing a stupid argument.

An educated person can use:

[
A \rightarrow B
]

without demonstrating the arrow.

A political scientist can confuse:

[
\text{event worth examining}
]

with:

[
\text{proof of preferred grand narrative}.
]

And expertise can make weak reasoning more persuasive because the speaker knows how to dress it properly.

The criticism therefore does not require:

Matt Goodwin has a low IQ.

It requires something much easier to test:

Matt Goodwin repeatedly publishes political claims whose rhetorical certainty exceeds the evidential machinery displayed beneath them.

That is a criticism of output.

And the output is sitting right there.

