20. Where Is the Fucking Policy?

This is the point at which political commentary and government diverge dramatically.

Suppose Britain really is being destroyed.

Fine.

What precisely happens Monday morning?

How many houses will be built?

Where?

Under what planning rules?

Who funds the infrastructure?

What happens to interest rates?

How much does the NHS receive?

How many doctors?

How are they trained?

What is the migration number?

How is it enforced?

How many civil servants administer the new system?

What happens to sectors dependent upon migrant labour?

What is the tax base?

What is the borrowing requirement?

What is the energy mix?

What happens to electricity prices?

What is the social-care settlement?

How does the government increase productivity?

Which regulations disappear?

What economic cost follows from removing them?

How do you reconcile promises that contradict one another?

This is government.

Government is an optimisation problem with millions of variables and millions of human beings embedded inside it.

Calling somebody a traitor is comparatively easy.

A nation cannot be administered through thumbnail captions.

