23. Discussion: The Politics of Permanent Emergency

Britain has serious problems.

Housing affordability.

Economic productivity.

Migration.

Energy.

Healthcare.

Regional inequality.

Public infrastructure.

Trust.

Crime.

Social cohesion.

State capacity.

Those problems are serious enough that they deserve better than being continuously compressed into:

YOU HAVE BEEN BETRAYED.

Because betrayal is not an economic model.

Anger is not public administration.

Fear is not a housing policy.

A viral post cannot run a hospital.

And identifying somebody to blame is not equivalent to knowing how to govern 70 million people.

The Permanent Emergency Machine solves a different optimisation problem.

It asks:

[
\text{What makes the audience return tomorrow?}
]

rather than:

[
\text{What makes Britain function better in twenty years?}
]

Those are very different objective functions.

