24. Conclusion

Matt Goodwin is valuable as a case study precisely because he occupies several roles simultaneously.

Scholar.

Pollster.

Commentator.

Campaigner.

Former Reform parliamentary candidate.

Subscription publisher.

Political advocate.

The problem is not that he possesses political opinions.

Everybody does.

The problem arises when advocacy inherits the authority of analysis while increasingly using the techniques of political mobilisation.

The evidence examined here reveals a recurrent rhetorical structure.

Events become emergencies.

Opponents become threats.

Electoral setbacks become evidence of deeper problems.

The Conservative Party becomes guilty of “betraying Britain”.

And the solution is Reform UK, whose eight current MPs all possess some form of previous Conservative Party history and five of whom actually sat as Conservative MPs.

The resulting political proposition is breathtakingly elegant:

[
\boxed{
\text{The Tories betrayed Britain.}
}
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{Vote for the other party full of Tories.}
}
]

No contradiction is detected because the crucial transformation has already occurred.

Yesterday’s Conservative politician becomes today’s anti-establishment insurgent merely by walking through a different door.

The ideas survive.

The personnel migrate.

The electorate is told that it was betrayed rather than invited to ask whether any part of the underlying programme itself failed.

And the political commentator who spent years professionally studying populist mobilisation now stands beside the machinery explaining that the establishment is collapsing, the country is being destroyed, the people are awakening and the next obscure council election may TERRIFY somebody.

Perhaps Matt Goodwin never abandoned political science.

Perhaps this is simply participant observation taken to an extraordinary extreme.

Either way, the machine continues.

BETRAYAL.

DESTRUCTION.

TERRIFY.

WAKE UP.

Subscribe below.

And somewhere behind the curtain, another former Tory is putting on a Reform rosette.

